🔐 Security Measures to Make a Kubernetes Pod More Secure
1. Run Container as Non-Root User

By default, kuch containers root user ke saath run kar sakte hain. Agar attacker container compromise kar le, toh root privileges dangerous ho sakti hain.

securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  runAsUser: 1000

Benefit: Container process root ke as privileges ke bina run karega.

2. Use Read-Only Root Filesystem

Container ke root filesystem ko read-only bana do:

securityContext:
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true

Isse attacker container ke filesystem mein malicious files modify/create nahi kar paayega.

Agar application ko temporary files chahiye:

volumeMounts:
  - name: tmp
    mountPath: /tmp

volumes:
  - name: tmp
    emptyDir: {}
3. Drop Linux Capabilities

Linux capabilities container ko additional privileges deti hain. Unnecessary capabilities remove karni chahiye.

securityContext:
  capabilities:
    drop:
      - ALL

Agar specifically koi capability required ho:

capabilities:
  drop:
    - ALL
  add:
    - NET_BIND_SERVICE

Principle: Sirf required privilege do.

4. Prevent Privilege Escalation

Container process ko additional privileges gain karne se prevent karo.

securityContext:
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false

Ye especially useful hai agar application compromise ho jaye.

5. Don't Use Privileged Containers

Avoid:

securityContext:
  privileged: true

privileged: true container ko host ke bahut powerful privileges de sakta hai.

❌ Avoid unless there is a genuine infrastructure-level requirement.

6. Use Seccomp

Seccomp Linux system calls ko restrict karta hai.

securityContext:
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault

Container sirf allowed system calls use kar sakega.

Simple way to remember:

Seccomp = Restrict what system calls a container can make.

7. Use AppArmor / SELinux

Linux security mechanisms jaise AppArmor aur SELinux application/container ke behavior ko restrict kar sakte hain.

For example, AppArmor can restrict things like:

File access
Network access
Certain system operations

Ye extra security layer provide karta hai.

8. Don't Store Secrets Directly in Image

❌ Don't do:

ENV DB_PASSWORD=mysecret123

Aur source code mein bhi:

const password = "mysecret123";

Instead Kubernetes Secret use karo:

env:
  - name: DB_PASSWORD
    valueFrom:
      secretKeyRef:
        name: db-secret
        key: password

For higher-security environments, external secret managers such as AWS Secrets Manager or HashiCorp Vault can also be used.

9. Use NetworkPolicy

By default, depending on the CNI/network configuration, pods may be able to communicate broadly.

NetworkPolicy se define kar sakte ho:

Kaunsa Pod kis Pod se communicate kar sakta hai?

Example:

apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: backend-policy
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: backend
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
    - from:
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: frontend

Meaning:

Frontend Pod
     |
     | allowed
     ↓
Backend Pod

Other Pods
     |
     X
     ↓
Backend
10. Use Resource Requests and Limits

Resource limits security ka direct replacement nahi hain, but resource exhaustion/DoS risk ko reduce karne mein help karte hain.

resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "100m"
    memory: "128Mi"

  limits:
    cpu: "500m"
    memory: "256Mi"

Agar application uncontrolled memory/CPU consume kare, toh poore node ko affect karne ka risk reduce hota hai.

11. Use Trusted Container Images

Random/untrusted images use mat karo.

Instead:

image: myregistry/myapp:v1.2.0

Aur image ko scan karo using tools such as:

Trivy
Docker Scout
AWS ECR scanning

Example:

trivy image myapp:v1.2.0
12. Don't Use latest Tag

Avoid:

image: myapp:latest

Better:

image: myapp:1.4.2

Ya preferably immutable image digests.

Why?

latest tag change ho sakta hai, so same Pod definition different image pull kar sakti hai.

13. Use Pod Security Standards

Kubernetes Pod Security Standards (PSS) provide three levels:

Privileged
    ↓
Baseline
    ↓
Restricted

Production workloads ke liye Restricted profile ka goal strong security controls enforce karna hai.

Example namespace level:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: production
  labels:
    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted
14. Use RBAC

Pod/application ko unnecessarily Kubernetes API permissions mat do.

For example, agar application ko Pods list karne ki zarurat nahi hai, toh usko permission mat do.

Follow:

Principle of Least Privilege

Matlab:

Required permission → YES
Unnecessary permission → NO
15. Secure Service Account

Agar Pod ko Kubernetes API access ki zarurat nahi hai:

spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false

Ye important hai because compromised Pod ke andar automatically available ServiceAccount token attacker ke liye useful ho sakta hai.

🧩 Production Security Example

Ek relatively secure Pod configuration kuch is type ki ho sakti hai:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: secure-app
spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false

  containers:
    - name: app
      image: myapp:1.0.0

      securityContext:
        runAsNonRoot: true
        runAsUser: 1000
        allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
        readOnlyRootFilesystem: true

        capabilities:
          drop:
            - ALL

        seccompProfile:
          type: RuntimeDefault

      resources:
        requests:
          cpu: "100m"
          memory: "128Mi"

        limits:
          cpu: "500m"
          memory: "256Mi"
